course: dictionary
lesson: 8149
title: What people think of you
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A reputation is what people generally think about someone or something, based on what they have done in the past. «A bad reputation can arrive before you, but it can be changed day by day.» So a reputation is built from past actions, and it can change.
explain: Use the words around a new word to find its meaning. «Jake asked, "why does everyone expect me to be the bad one?"» Everyone expects Jake to be bad, so reputation must mean the opinion people hold about him. Try it: "what people think of Jake" fits the sentence.
explain: Reputations take time. «Reputations take time to change, and Mr Pryce promised always to ask Jake's side first.» One bad week is not a whole reputation, but a moment repeated «always» can build one.
fact: A reputation is what people generally think about someone or something, based on what they have done in the past.
fact: A reputation for something means being known for it, good or bad.
fact: Phrases to know: «a good reputation», «damage your reputation», «live up to your reputation».
fact: A good reputation takes years to build, and it can be lost quickly.
fact: Lying and cheating damage your reputation.
exercise: «The shop has a reputation for friendly staff.» What does this mean? | *It is known for having friendly staff | It has a sign that says hello | It was built many years ago | It sells friendly staff to other shops
exercise: Ella says, «Lying and cheating damage your reputation.» What does damage mean here? | *Harm, so people think less well of you | Paint over, so it looks new | Hide it in a box | Make it louder and bigger
exercise: Which phrase means someone is known for something bad? | a good reputation | *a reputation for cheating | a cup of tea | live up to your reputation
test: «Reputations take time to change.» What does this mean? | Everyone changes their name quickly | Reputations are made of paper and can be torn | *What people think of someone changes slowly | Time stops for reputations
test: Why might Mr Pryce ask Jake's side first? | Because he likes long talks | *To be fair, so one story does not decide Jake's reputation | To get Jake into trouble | To prove Jake is always wrong
exam: «A reputation can grow from one small moment repeated as always.» What does this suggest? | Reputations are only about money | Reputations never change | A reputation is built from one moment only | *A reputation is built from many repeated actions, not one moment
